{
  "configuration_id": "config_038",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best config (config_036: pca_n2000 + resnet50 + avgpool + weighted, acc=0.639) shows that adding image modality to RNA helps (~5 pts over RNA-only), weighted fusion beats concat, avgpool beats transformer aggregation, and resnet50 marginally edges ViT-L. However, all experiments so far use pca_n2000 for RNA—a simple linear projection. The RNA encoder is the largest untested design axis. Question: Does a learned nonlinear RNA representation (scvi_n200) improve upon PCA when all other components are held at their empirically best settings? config_038 changes only the RNA encoder from pca_n2000 to scvi_n200 relative to config_036, cleanly isolating the representation effect. Why this is the best next experiment: It is a single-variable comparison against our strongest baseline, directly tests whether the RNA bottleneck is representation quality (linear vs. learned), and the answer will determine whether to invest remaining budget in more complex RNA encoders (scgpt) or instead explore the profile-setting axis. If scvi_n200 matches or beats PCA, it validates learned representations and motivates testing scgpt; if it underperforms, PCA suffices and we should pivot to profile setting or image-encoder refinements."
}

